 1 big thing: Intelligence warns of &quot;advanced&quot; Iranian plots 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Obtained by Axios 

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 U.S. intelligence finds Iran has "substantially increased" its efforts to target American diplomats and officials in the Middle East and beyond, with lethal plots reaching "advanced stages," according to a memo yesterday to Secretary of State Marco Rubio, obtained by Axios' Barak Ravid. 

 Why it matters: This is more evidence the war is likely to escalate after midterms on Nov. 3, 10 days from now. News of the memo exploded across the internet after Axios posted it last night, with media coverage worldwide . U.S. officials say the intelligence is one reason the State Department is drawing down personnel at American embassies across the Middle East, amid mounting fears of a broader regional conflict.

 U.S. officials also fear that Iran-backed Houthi rebels in Yemen could expand their attacks beyond Saudi Arabia to other countries in the region. Adding to the volatile security environment is the prospect of renewed U.S. and Israeli military operations against Iran, potentially after the midterm elections. President Trump said at a rally in Fort Myers, Fla., yesterday: "We may do another little Operation Epic Fury" unless the Iranians "will agree" to a deal. 

 "If they're smart, they will just say: 'Let's get down to business.' But I doubt they will do that because perhaps they're not smart." ✈️ State of play: Earlier this week, the State Department ordered family members and nonessential personnel to leave the U.S. Embassy in Riyadh and consulates in Jeddah and Dhahran.

 Yesterday, the department also ordered reductions in U.S. diplomatic and government personnel at embassies in the UAE, Lebanon, Qatar and Jordan, a U.S. official said. Share this story . 

 
 
