 2. 🤫 Washington&#x27;s message to AI execs: Please shut up 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Washington has always viewed Silicon Valley as culturally strange. But lawmakers and White House advisers see the current crop of AI leaders as a monumental political liability, Axios' Maria Curi and Stef W. Kight report. 

 Why it matters: Even pro-AI government officials and lawmakers wish AI founders would do what every other major industry chief does — put a sock in it and just build their products ... safely. 🚨 State of play: Rather than leading the charge to persuade Americans about the benefits of AI and the potential for shared prosperity — core elements of a winning campaign — industry leaders constantly warn of existential dangers. 

 Lawmakers say the message from AI execs too often alternates between prosperity and doom, sometimes in the same public appearances. 🏛️ The whiplash adds to political instability in the face of an already powerful public backlash, lawmakers and regulators tell us:

 Sen. John Curtis (R-Utah) told Axios: "It's obvious that they're tripping over their own shoelaces in trying to sell the value of this to the public." Rep. Ro Khanna (D-Calif.), who represents part of Silicon Valley, told Axios: "You cannot trust billionaires to make decisions to keep us safe." Sen. Bernie Moreno (R-Ohio) complained about "panic-inducing commentary" in a letter to Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei earlier this month: "Your instinct is to wreak havoc on the ... discourse." Sen. Chris Murphy (D-Conn.) told Axios: "Democrats need to make the AI industry's political money an issue, data centers an issue, and go on offense to protect workers and kids." 👀 Between the lines: Friday night news dumps and consequential weekend blog posts have become bread and butter for Anthropic and OpenAI. 

 Regulators are left wondering whether that's a feature or a bug: deliberate attempts to quietly course-correct damaging narratives ahead of IPOs, or the work of inexperienced teams struggling to navigate Washington. Share this story . 

 
 
